==Geography==
[[File:Indiarivers.png|thumb|Rivers of India]]

India is the seventh biggest country in the world by land. It is the main part of the [[Indian subcontinent]]. The [[Borders of the Republic of India|countries next to India]] are [[Pakistan]], [[Bangladesh]], [[Myanmar]], [[China]], [[Bhutan]] and [[Nepal]]. It is also near [[Sri Lanka]] and the [[Maldives]], two [[island country|island countries]]. The [[Andaman and Nicobar Islands]], a [[union territory]] of India, is near [[Thailand]], [[Indonesia]] and Myanmar.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Andaman and Nicobar Islands · India|url=https://www.google.com/maps/place/Andaman+and+Nicobar+Islands,+India/@10.213225,91.00633,7z/data=!3m1!4b1!4m5!3m4!1s0x3064a00f2b650ff3:0xce80055648fccb2c!8m2!3d11.7400867!4d92.6586401?hl=en|access-date=3 June 2021|website=Andaman and Nicobar Islands · India|language=en-AU}}</ref>

=== Terrain ===
There are many mountains in the northern part of India. There is the [[Himalayas]], which have some of the tallest mountains in the world. 

There are many rivers in India. The main rivers are the [[Ganges]], the [[Brahmaputra River|Brahmaputra]], the [[Yamuna]], the Godavari, the Kaveri, the Narmada, and the Krishna.

=== Coastline ===
India is a [[peninsula]], which means that it is surrounded on three sides by water. In the west is the [[Arabian Sea]], in the south is the Indian Ocean, and in the east is the [[Bay of Bengal]].

India's total [[coastline]] is {{Convert|7517|km|mi|-2|adj=on|abbr=off}} long.<ref name="sanilkumar">{{cite journal|last1=Kumar|first1=V. Sanil|last2=Pathak|first2=K. C.|last3=Pednekar|first3=P.|last4=Raju|first4=N. S. N.|year=2006|title=Coastal processes along the Indian coastline|url=http://drs.nio.org/drs/bitstream/2264/350/1/Curr_Sci_91_530.pdf|journal=Current Science|volume=91|issue=4|pages=530–536}}</ref> The mainland's coastline is {{Convert|5423|km|mi|-2|adj=on|abbr=off}} long. The [[Andaman Islands|Andaman]], [[Nicobar Islands|Nicobar]] and [[Lakshadweep]] islands have {{Convert|2094|km|mi|-2|adj=on|abbr=off}} long coastlines in total.{{sfn|Kumar|Pathak|Pednekar|Raju|2006}} 

Based on [[Indian Navy|Indian naval]] [[hydrography|hydrographic]] [[chart]]s, 43% of the mainland coast are [[Sand|sandy]] [[Beach|beaches]], 11% are rocky shores and [[Cliff|cliffs]], and 46% are [[mudflat]]s or [[marsh]]y shores.{{sfn|Kumar|Pathak|Pednekar|Raju|2006}}

=== Climates ===
India has different climates.<ref name="CONCISE ENCYCLOPEDIA 4">{{cite book
|title = Concise Encyclopedia
|publisher = Dorling Kindersley Limited
|year = 1997
|pages = 333
|isbn = 0-7513-5911-4}}</ref> In [[South India]], the climate is mainly [[tropics|tropical]], which means it can get very hot in summer and cool in winter.<ref name="CONCISE ENCYCLOPEDIA 4" /> The northern part, though, has a cooler climate, called [[subtropics|subtropical]]. The mountainous regions can be [[alpine climate|alpine]].<ref name="CONCISE ENCYCLOPEDIA 4" /> The [[Himalayas]], in the alpine climate region, can get extremely cold.  

The Himalayas block cold Central Asian winds from blowing into the [[Indian subcontinent]]. It keeps the most of the subcontinent warmer than most places at same [[latitude]]s.{{sfn|Posey|1994|p = 118}}{{sfn|Wolpert|2003|p = 4}} 

There is very heavy rainfall along the west coast and in the Eastern Himalayan foothills. The west, though, is drier. 

==== Monsoon ====
Because of some of India's [[desert]]s, the entire country gets rain for four months of the year. That time (usually around June to September) is called the [[monsoon]]. It happens because the deserts attract water-filled winds from the Indian Ocean, which give rain when they come into India. 

When the monsoon rains come late or not so heavily, [[Drought|droughts]] (when the land dries out because there is less rain) are possible.

==Politics==
{{multiple image
|perrow=1
|total_width=250
|image1=Rashtrapati Bhavan Wide New Delhi India.jpg
|caption1=The [[Rashtrapati Bhavan]] in [[New Delhi]] is the official residence (house where a person lives) of the [[President of India]].
|image2=Glimpses of the new Parliament Building, in New Delhi (2).jpg
|caption2=The [[New Parliament House, New Delhi|Parliament House]] (''Sansad Bhavan'') in [[New Delhi]] is the place where the [[Parliament of India]] meets. 
