India is a [[parliamentary republic]] with a [[multi-party system]].{{sfn|Burnell|Calvert|1999|p = 125}} It is the largest democracy in the world by the number of people.<ref name="largestdem1">{{cite web
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/world/south_asia/country_profiles/1154019.stm
| title = Country profile: India
| accessdate= 14 March 2019
| date = 19 April 2012
| publisher = BBC
}}</ref> It has six{{nbsp}}[[List of political parties in India|national parties]], for example the [[Indian National Congress]] (INC) and the [[Bharatiya Janata Party]] (BJP). It also has more than 50{{nbsp}}[[List of political parties in India|regional parties]].{{sfn|Election Commission of India}} The Congress is known as [[Centrism|centre]] in Indian [[political culture]],<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Sáez |first1=Lawrence |last2=Sinha |first2=Aseema |year=2010 |title=Political cycles, political institutions and public expenditure in India, 1980–2000 |url=https://archive.org/details/sim_british-journal-of-political-science_2010-01_40_1/page/91 |journal=[[British Journal of Political Science]] |volume=40 |issue=1 |pages=91–113 |doi=10.1017/s0007123409990226 |s2cid=154767259 |issn = 0007-1234}}</ref> while the BJP is known as [[Right-wing politics|right-wing]].{{sfn|Malik|Singh|1992|pp=318–336}}{{sfn|Banerjee|2005|p=3118}}<ref>{{Cite news |last=Halarnkar |first=Samar |date=13 June 2012 |title=Narendra Modi makes his move |work=[[BBC News]] |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-asia-india-18352532 |quote=The right-wing Hindu nationalist Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP), India's primary opposition party}}</ref> The Congress was the [[wikt:majority|majority]] in the [[Parliament of India|Parliament]] from 1950 to the end of the 1980s. From the end of the 1980s, the BJP{{sfn|Sarkar|2007|p=84}} and the powerful regional parties are getting more seats in the Parliament over time. This forced the national parties to create [[coalition government]]s.{{sfn|Chander|2004|p=117}}

===Government===
{{Main|Government of India|Constitution of India}}

India is ruled under the Constitution of India. It is the country's highest [[wikt:document|document]] of law. It came into effect on 26 January 1950.{{sfn|Pylee|2003a|p = 4}} Its original form said that India would be a "[[Sovereignty|sovereign]], [[liberal democracy|democratic]] [[republic]]". In 1971, the statement was changed to "sovereign, [[socialist]], [[Secularism|secular]], democratic republic".{{sfn|Dutt|1998|p = 421}} 

India has been said to be a "quasi-[[federation|federal]]" form of government. That means a strong [[federal government]] with weak state governments.{{sfn|Wheare|1980|p = 28}} The federal government is often called the "union government" or the "central government" but after political, economic and social changes at the end of the 1990s, the government became federal.{{sfn|Echeverri-Gent|2002|pp = 19–20}}{{sfn|Sinha|2004|p = 25}}

==== Branches of government ====
The union government is divided into three parts: the [[legislature]] (the one that make laws), the [[executive (government)|executive]] (the one that applies laws), and the [[judiciary]] (the one that makes sure that the laws are obeyed).<ref name="constitution">[[s:Constitution of India (2020)|Constitution of India]]</ref> All three parts are in [[New Delhi]], the [[capital city]] of India.

The legislature of India is called the [[Parliament of India|Parliament]] ({{lang|hi|संसद}} {{transl|hi|ISO|Sansad}}). It is divided into two houses: the upper house [[Rajya Sabha]] (Council of States); and the lower house [[Lok Sabha]] (House of the People).{{sfn|Gledhill|1970|p = 127}} The Rajya Sabha has 245 members. They remain members for six years.{{sfn|Sharma|2007|p = 161}} Most members are [[election|elected]] indirectly by the legislatures of [[States and union territories of India|state and union territories]].{{sfn|Sharma|2007|p = 162}} The Lok Sabha has 545 members. They remain members for five years. They are elected directly by the people's vote.{{sfn|Sharma|2007|p = 143}}

The executive is made up of the [[President of India|President]], the [[Vice President of India|Vice President]], the [[Prime Minister of India|Prime Minister]] and the [[Union Council of Ministers]]. The President is the [[head of state]] of India.{{sfn|Sharma|2007|p = 31}} The presidents are elected by an [[electoral college (India)|electoral college]] for a period of five years. The electoral college is made up of members of central and state legislatures.{{sfn|Sharma|2007|p = 138}}{{sfn|Gledhill|1970|p = 112}} The Prime Minister is the [[head of government]] of India. The President can choose the Prime Minister, who has most of the power.{{sfn|Sharma|2007|p = 162}} The President has less power than the Prime Minister. The Union Council of Ministers helps the Prime Minister. It is similar to a [[cabinet (government)|cabinet]] in many countries.

The judicial branch is made up of three types of [[court of law|courts of law]]: the [[Supreme Court of India|Supreme Court]], the 24 [[High Courts of India|High Courts]] and a number of trial courts.{{sfn|Neuborne|2003|p = 478}} The [[Chief Justice of India]] is the head of the Supreme Court. The members of the court have the power to stop a law being passed by Parliament if they think that the law contradicts (opposes) the Constitution.{{sfn|Sripati|1998|pp=423–424}} They can make any government action [[wikt:invalid|invalid]] if it contradicts the Constitution.{{sfn|Pylee|2003b|p = 314}}

===Divisions===
For [[administration]] purposes, India has been divided into smaller pieces. Most of these pieces are called [[state]]s; others are called [[Union territory|union territories]]. In total, there are twenty-eight states and eight union territories.<ref name="LOC PROFILE"/>

States and union territories differ in the way they are represented. Most union territories are ruled by administrators (called Lieutenant Governors) sent by the central government. All the states (and the territories of Delhi and Puducherry) [[election|elect]] their local government themselves. 

