[[File:Jupiter diagram.svg|thumb|300px|Diagram of Jupiter]]
[[File:790106-0203 Voyager 58M to 31M reduced.gif|thumb|A series of photographs taken by [[Voyager 1]] as it approached Jupiter, made into an [[animation]]]]

'''Jupiter''' is the largest [[planet]] in the [[Solar System]]. It is the fifth planet from the [[Sun]].<ref name=what>{{cite web |url=https://www.nasa.gov/audience/forstudents/k-4/stories/what-is-jupiter-k4.html#.VRt-v45Nhaw |title=What Is Jupiter? |first= |last= |work=NASA |year=2014 |accessdate=April 1, 2015 |archive-date=March 18, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150318040645/https://www.nasa.gov/audience/forstudents/k-4/stories/what-is-jupiter-k4.html#.VRt-v45Nhaw |url-status=live }}</ref> Jupiter is a [[gas giant]] because it is large and made mostly of [[gas]]. Gas giants are a type of [[giant planet]]s.<ref>Williams, David R. (December 23, 2021). "Jupiter Fact Sheet". NASA. [https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/jupiterfact.html] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110413155109/http://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/jupiterfact.html|date=April 13, 2011}}</ref> The other gas [[giant planet]]s in the Solar System are [[Saturn (planet)|Saturn]], [[Uranus (planet)|Uranus]] and [[Neptune (planet)|Neptune]].

Jupiter was discovered by [[Galileo Galilei]] in 1610 with a small telescope. The planet has a [[Great Red Spot]] which is located at 22 degrees south of Jupiter's equator. The great red spot has wind-speeds up to 432 km/h (268 mph). 

By mass, [[Jupiter's atmosphere]] is around 76% [[hydrogen]] and 24% [[helium]]. However, since helium atoms are larger than [[hydrogen]] molecules, Jupiter's upper atmosphere is about 90% hydrogen and 10% helium by volume. The [[atmosphere]] also contains small amounts of [[methane]], [[water vapor|water vapour]], [[ammonia]], and [[silicon]]-based compounds as well as trace amounts of [[carbon]], [[ethane]], [[hydrogen sulfide]], [[neon]], [[oxygen]], [[phosphine]], and [[sulfur]]. The outermost layer of the atmosphere contains [[crystals]] of frozen ammonia. Through [[infrared]] and [[ultraviolet]] measurements, small amounts of benzene and other hydrocarbons have also been found. The interior of Jupiter contains denser materials—by mass it is roughly 71% hydrogen, 24% helium, and 5% other elements.

Jupiter was the first planet to form. It moved towards the center during the early times of the Solar System. This affected how other planets were formed. Hydrogen makes up most of Jupiter (90% by volume). Helium is the second and makes up a quarter of the mass and a tenth of the volume. Jupiter's interior is getting smaller. This process makes more heat than the planet receives from the Sun. It is believed that there is an outer mantle and a diffuse inner core of denser material inside Jupiter. Liquid metallic hydrogen would make up the outer mantle. Jupiter rotates very fast at 1 rotation per 10 hours. This creates a slight but noticeable bulge around the equator, therefore Jupiter's shape is an oblate spheroid. The outer atmosphere has many bands across the latitude. Interactions between the bands create turbulence and storms. The Great Red Spot is the most obvious example. It is a giant storm which has been seen since 1831,  and possibly earlier.

== Name and symbol ==
[[File:Jupiter symbol (late classical and medieval mss).png|left|thumb|The symbol for Jupiter in late Classical (4th c.) and [[Middle Ages|medieval]] [[Byzantine Empire|Byzantine]] (11th c.) [[Manuscript|manuscripts]] comes from [[Ζ]] (zeta).<ref name="jones-1999">{{cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=8MokzymQ43IC|title=Astronomical papyri from Oxyrhynchus|last=Jones|first=Alexander|date=1999|isbn=9780871692337|pages=62–63|publisher=American Philosophical Society |quote=It is now possible to trace the medieval symbols for at least four of the five planets to forms that occur in some of the latest papyrus horoscopes ([[Oxyrhynchus Papyri|P.Oxy.]]  4272, 4274, 4275 [...]). That for Jupiter is an obvious monogram derived from the initial letter of the Greek name.|access-date=March 23, 2022|archive-date=April 30, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210430052052/https://books.google.com/books?id=8MokzymQ43IC|url-status=live}}</ref>]]
Jupiter was named for the king of the [[gods]]. The [[Greeks]] called him [[Zeus]]. The Romans called him [[Jupiter (mythology)|Jupiter]]. The symbol for Jupiter, [[:File:Jupiter symbol (fixed width).svg|♃]], is from the Greek [[zeta]]. It has a horizontal stroke {{angbr|Ƶ}}. This stands as an [[abbreviation]] for ''Zeus''.<ref name="jones-1999" /><ref>{{cite journal|last=Maunder|first=A.S.D.|date=1934|title=The origin of the symbols of the planets|journal=The Observatory|volume=57|pages=238–247|bibcode=1934Obs....57..238M}}</ref>{{Page needed|date=June 2023}}<ref>{{cite book|url=https://www.etymonline.com/word/jove|title=Jove|last=Harper|first=Douglas|work=Online Etymology Dictionary|access-date=March 22, 2022|archive-date=March 23, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220323021235/https://www.etymonline.com/word/jove|url-status=live}}</ref>

== Structure ==
Jupiter is the biggest planet in the Solar System. Its [[wikt:diameter|diameter]] is 142,984&nbsp;[[km]] (88846 mi), eleven times larger than the [[diameter]] of  Earth.<ref name=coffey>{{cite web |url=https://www.universetoday.com/22710/jupiter-compared-to-earth/ |title=Jupiter Compared to Earth |first=Jerry |last=Coffey |work=universetoday.com |date=December 22, 2008|accessdate=April 1, 2015}}</ref> Jupiter is twice as [[mass]]ive as all the other planets in the Solar System combined.<ref name="hamilton">{{cite web|last=Hamilton|first=Calvin|year=2015|title=The Solar System|url=https://solarviews.com/eng/solarsys.htm|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150417194854/https://solarviews.com/eng/solarsys.htm|archive-date=April 17, 2015|work=solarviews.com|accessdate=April 1, 2015}}</ref> Jupiter is 318 times as massive as Earth. The [[volume]] of Jupiter is 1,317 times the volume of Earth. In other words, 1,317 Earth-sized objects could fit inside it.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://er.jsc.nasa.gov/SEH/jupiter.html|title=Jupiter|publisher=NASA|accessdate=July 20, 2009|archive-date=May 26, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526124127/https://er.jsc.nasa.gov/SEH/jupiter.html|url-status=live}}</ref> The planet gives off more [[heat]] than it gets from the [[Sun]].<ref>{{cite book| first=Linda T. | last=Elkins-Tanton | year=2006| title=Jupiter and Saturn | url=https://archive.org/details/jupitersaturn0000elki_t3r5 | publisher=Chelsea House| location=New York | isbn=978-0-8160-5196-0 }}</ref>{{page needed|date=July 2023}}

[[File:PIA02863 - Jupiter surface motion animation.gif|thumb|Animation of part of Jupiter's cloud system. The animation is made of over 40 [[photograph]]s taken by the [[Cassini-Huygens|Cassini]] [[spacecraft]] between October 31 and November 9, 2000.]]
