=== Atmosphere ===
The [[atmosphere]] near the [[wikt:surface|surface]] of Jupiter is about 90% [[hydrogen]], 10% [[helium]], and less than 1% other gases.<ref name=factsheet />

The lower atmosphere is so heated and the [[pressure]] so high that helium [[condensation|changes to liquid]], which then [[rain]]s down onto the planet.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://ael.gsfc.nasa.gov/jupiterHighlights.shtml |title=Highlights of the Galileo Probe Mass Spectrometer Investigation |accessdate=August 23, 2013 |format= |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090410221648/https://ael.gsfc.nasa.gov/jupiterHighlights.shtml |archive-date=April 10, 2009 }}</ref> Based on [[spectroscopy]], Jupiter seems to be made up of the same gases as [[Saturn (planet)|Saturn]]. It is different from [[Neptune (planet)|Neptune]] or [[Uranus (planet)|Uranus]]. Those two planets have much less hydrogen and helium gas.<ref>{{cite web |author=Ingersoll, A. P. |author2=Hammel, H. B. |author3=Spilker, T. R. |author4=Young, R. E. |date=2005 |url=https://www.lpi.usra.edu/opag/outer_planets.pdf |title=Outer Planets: The Ice Giants |publisher=Lunar & Planetary Institute |accessdate=February 1, 2007|archive-date=December 26, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181226062359/https://www.lpi.usra.edu/opag/outer_planets.pdf%0a%20 |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Core ===
It is not possible to say exactly what metals are in the core of Jupiter. However, by measuring the [[gravity]] around Jupiter, one can estimate its size. The inner core is dense. It has a lot of heavy elements, likely in the form of rock and ice. The heavy elements in the core have a total mass of 7–25 times that of Earth.<ref>{{Cite book|title=Astrophysics of planet formation|last=Armitage|first=Philip J.|date=2020|isbn=978-1-108-34422-7|edition=2nd|location=Cambridge|pages=239–240|oclc=1122692088}}</ref>

Round the unknown inner core is an outer core. The outer core of Jupiter is thick, liquid hydrogen.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.universetoday.com/14470/does-jupiter-have-a-solid-core/ |title=Does Jupiter Have a Solid Core? |first=Jerry |last=Coffey |work=universetoday.com |date=May 22, 2008 |accessdate=April 1, 2015 |archive-date=April 29, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150429191859/https://www.universetoday.com/14470/does-jupiter-have-a-solid-core/ |url-status=live }}</ref> 

Jupiter is mainly made of the same elements (hydrogen and helium) as the [[Sun]], but it is not large enough to have the internal pressure and temperature necessary to cause hydrogen to [[Nuclear fusion|fuse]] to helium, the energy source that powers the Sun and most other stars. If Jupiter had 75 times its mass, it could fuse hydrogen to helium.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://astronomy.com/magazine/greatest-mysteries/2019/07/48-is-jupiter-a-failed-star|title=Is Jupiter a Failed Star?|access-date=January 4, 2021|archive-date=January 13, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210113053325/https://astronomy.com/magazine/greatest-mysteries/2019/07/48-is-jupiter-a-failed-star|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Cloud layers ===
Jupiter has many bands of clouds going [[wikt:horizontal|horizontally]] across its surface. The lighter areas are zones and the darker ones are belts. The zones and belts often [[wikt:interaction|interact]] with each other. This causes huge [[storm]]s. [[Wind]] speeds of 360&nbsp;[[kilometres per hour]] (km/h) are common on Jupiter.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.lpl.arizona.edu/~showman/publications/ingersolletal-2004.pdf |title=Dynamics of Jupiter's atmosphere |accessdate=July 20, 2009 |format= |publisher=The University of Arizona |archive-date=May 14, 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110514221437/https://www.lpl.arizona.edu/~showman/publications/ingersolletal-2004.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref> To show the difference, the strongest [[tropical storm]]s on Earth are about 100&nbsp;km/h.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://old.weathersa.co.za/References/Cyclones.jsp|title=Tropical cyclones|publisher=Weather South Africa|accessdate=July 19, 2009|archiveurl=https://www.webcitation.org/652Gwj0pZ?url=https://old.weathersa.co.za/References/Cyclones.jsp|archivedate=January 28, 2012|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Most of the clouds on Jupiter are made of [[ammonia]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.solarviews.com/eng/jupiter.htm|title=Jupiter|accessdate=July 20, 2009|publisher=Solar View|archive-date=May 26, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526124126/https://solarviews.com/eng/jupiter.htm|url-status=live}}</ref> There may also be clouds of [[water vapour|water vapor]], like the clouds on Earth. Multiple spacecraft such as Voyager&nbsp;1 have seen [[lightning]] on the surface of the planet. Scientists think it was water vapor because lightning needs water vapor.<ref name=nasagal /> These lightning bolts have been measured as up to 1,000 times as powerful as those on Earth.<ref name=nasagal>{{cite web |url=https://www.nasa.gov/vision/universe/solarsystem/galileo_end.html |title=Surprising Jupiter - Busy Galileo spacecraft showed jovian system is full of surprises |accessdate=July 20, 2009 |format= |publisher=NASA |archive-date=October 8, 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111008010724/https://www.nasa.gov/vision/universe/solarsystem/galileo_end.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Great Red Spot ===
{{Main|Great Red Spot}}
One of the biggest features in Jupiter's atmosphere is the [[Great Red Spot]]. It is a huge storm that is bigger than the entire [[Earth]]. It is on record since at least 1831,<ref>{{cite journal|last=Denning|first=W. F.|title=Jupiter, early history of the great red spot on|journal=Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society|date=1899|volume=59|pages=574–584|bibcode=1899MNRAS..59..574D|doi=10.1093/mnras/59.10.574 |doi-access=free }}</ref>{{Page needed|date=June 2023}} and as early as 1665.<ref name="kyrala26">{{cite journal|last = Kyrala|first = A.|title=An explanation of the persistence of the Great Red Spot of Jupiter|journal=Moon and the Planets|date=1982|volume=26|issue = 1|pages=105–7|bibcode=1982M&P....26..105K|doi=10.1007/BF00941374|s2cid = 121637752}}</ref><ref>[https://www.gutenberg.org/files/28758/28758-h/28758-h.htm Philosophical Transactions Vol. I] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304001941/https://www.gutenberg.org/files/28758/28758-h/28758-h.htm |date=March 4, 2016 }} (1665–1666.). Project Gutenberg. Retrieved on December 22, 2011.</ref> Images by the [[Hubble Space Telescope]] have shown as many as two smaller "red spots" next to the Great Red Spot.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://hubblesite.org/newscenter/archive/releases/2008/23/image/a/|title=HubbleSite- NewsCenter|publisher=[[NASA]]|accessdate=December 12, 2013|archive-date=December 16, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131216055125/https://hubblesite.org/newscenter/archive/releases/2008/23/image/a/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://hubblesite.org/newscenter/archive/releases/2008/27/image/a/|title=HubbleSite- NewsCenter|publisher=[[NASA]]|accessdate=April 26, 2015|archive-date=May 1, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150501093610/https://hubblesite.org/newscenter/archive/releases/2008/27/image/a/|url-status=live}}</ref> Storms can last for hours or as long as hundreds of years, as in the case of the Great Red Spot.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.space.com/scienceastronomy/060504_red_jr.html|title=New Storm on Jupiter Hints at Climate Change|date=May 4, 2006|publisher=Space.com|accessdate=July 26, 2009|archive-date=July 15, 2007|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070715235623/https://www.space.com/scienceastronomy/060504_red_jr.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://pds.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/captions/jupiter/febgrs.htm|publisher=NASA|title=Great Red Spot|accessdate=July 26, 2009|archive-date=May 26, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526124127/https://pds.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/captions/jupiter/febgrs.htm|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Magnetic field ===
Jupiter has a [[magnetic field]] like Earth's but 10 times stronger.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://csep10.phys.utk.edu/astr161/lect/jupiter/magnetic.html|title=The Magnetic Field of Jupiter|publisher=AstroWiki|accessdate=July 21, 2009|archive-date=July 6, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160706132403/https://csep10.phys.utk.edu/astr161/lect/jupiter/magnetic.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> It also has a ''[[magnetosphere]]'' much bigger and stronger than Earth's. The field traps radiation belts much stronger than Earth's [[Van Allen radiation belt]]s, strong enough to endanger any spacecraft travelling near. The magnetic field is probably caused by the large amounts of liquid [[metal]]lic hydrogen in the core of Jupiter.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://cseligman.com/text/planets/metallichydrogen.htm |title=Planetary Magnetic Fields and Metallic Hydrogen |first=Courtney |last=Seligman |work=cseligman.com |year=2014 |accessdate=April 1, 2015 |archive-date=March 24, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150324033606/https://cseligman.com/text/planets/metallichydrogen.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> The four largest moons of Jupiter and many of the smaller ones [[orbit]] or go around the planet within the magnetic field. This protects them from the [[solar wind]]. Jupiter's magnetic field is so large, it reaches the orbit of [[Saturn (planet)|Saturn]] 7.7&nbsp;million&nbsp;[[miles]] (12&nbsp;million&nbsp;km) away.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.astrophysicsspectator.com/topics/planets/JupiterMagnetosphere.html |title=Jupiter's Magnetosphere |accessdate=July 20, 2009 |format= |publisher=Astrophysics Spectator |archive-date=June 12, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200612233814/https://www.astrophysicsspectator.com/topics/planets/JupiterMagnetosphere.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The Earth's magnetosphere does not even cover its [[moon]], less than a quarter of a million miles (400,000&nbsp;km) away. Jupiter also experiences large [[Aurora|aurorae]], which happen when charged particles from the volcanic moon [[Io]] land in [[Atmosphere of Jupiter|its atmosphere]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Mathewson|first=Samantha|date=February 9, 2022|title=Jupiter's auroras arise from a magnetic 'tug-of-war' with volcanic eruptions on its moon Io|url=https://www.space.com/jupiter-auroras-volcanic-moon-io-tug-of-war|access-date=April 4, 2023|website=Space.com}}</ref>

=== Ring system ===
{{main article|Rings of Jupiter}}
Jupiter also has a thin [[planetary ring]] system.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nasa.gov/pdf/316306main_JunoFactSheet_2009sm.pdf|page=2|title=Juno Mission to Jupiter|publisher=NASA|accessdate=July 21, 2011|archive-date=December 25, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181225085053/https://www.nasa.gov/pdf/316306main_JunoFactSheet_2009sm.pdf%20|url-status=live}}</ref> These rings are difficult to see and were not discovered until 1979 by [[NASA]]'s Voyager&nbsp;1 probe.<ref name=nsse/> There are four parts to Jupiter's rings. The closest ring to Jupiter is called the Halo Ring.<ref name="roj">{{cite web |url=https://www.windows2universe.org/jupiter/rings.html&edu=high |title=The Rings of Jupiter - Halo, Main and Gossamer RIng - Composition |first=Randy |last=Russell |work=windows2universe.org |year=2012 |accessdate=January 24, 2012 |archive-date=November 19, 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101119100857/https://www.windows2universe.org/jupiter/rings.html%26edu%3Dhigh |url-status=live }}</ref> The next ring is called the Main Ring. It is about {{convert|6440|km|mi|0|lk=on|abbr=out}} wide and only {{convert|30|km|mi|0|lk=in}} thick.<ref name="roj"/> The Main and Halo rings of Jupiter are made of small, dark particles.<ref name=nsse>{{cite web|url=https://sse.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/profile.cfm?Object=Jupiter&Display=OverviewLong|title=NASA: Solar System Exploration: Planets: Jupiter: Read More|publisher=NASA|accessdate=April 24, 2011|archive-date=September 20, 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110920121627/https://sse.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/profile.cfm?Object=Jupiter&Display=OverviewLong|url-status=dead}}</ref> The third and fourth rings, called the ''Gossamer'' rings, are [[transparent]] and are made from [[microscopic]] debris and dust.<ref name=nsse/> This dust probably comes from small meteors striking the surface of Jupiter's moons. The third ring is called the Amalthea Gossamer Ring, named after the moon [[Amalthea (moon)|Amalthea]]. The outer ring, the Thebe Gossamer Ring, is named after the moon [[Thebe (moon)|Thebe]]. The outer edge of this ring is about {{convert|220000|km|mi}} from Jupiter.<ref name="roj"/>

== Formation ==
Jupiter and other gas giants probably started as rocky planets, similar to Earth. This theory is called the ''core accretion model''.<ref>Kruijer, Thomas S.; Burkhardt, Christoph; Budde, Gerrit; Kleine, Thorsten 2017. Age of Jupiter inferred from the distinct genetics and formation times of meteorites. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences. 114 (26): 6712–6716. Bibcode:2017PNAS..114.6712K. doi:10.1073/pnas.1704461114. PMC 5495263. PMID 28607079</ref> The rocky core would have formed in the early Solar System, within a [[Protoplanetary disk|disk of gases]] around the Sun. When the planet reached a critical mass, its gravity started to quickly capture lots of gas. In this way, Jupiter became a giant planet. In order for Jupiter to reach this critical mass before the gas disk disappeared, there must have been lots of ice in the area. Jupiter must have formed outside the snow line, the area that is cold enough for water to freeze.<ref name="Armitage" /><ref>In astronomy or planetary science, the frost line, also known as the snow line or ice line, is the minimum distance from the central protostar of a solar nebula where the temperature is low enough for volatile compounds such as water, ammonia, methane, carbon dioxide and carbon monoxide to condense into solid grains, which will allow their accretion into planetesimals.</ref>

The ''disk instability model'' is another theory. It says that Jupiter was formed by gas clumping together in the disk around the Sun. In this case, a rocky core would not need to form. However, this process would probably create planets that are bigger than Jupiter, so most scientists think Jupiter was formed by core accretion.<ref name="Armitage">{{Cite book|title=Astrophysics of planet formation|last=Armitage|first=Philip J.|date=2020|isbn=978-1-108-34422-7|edition=2nd|location=Cambridge|pages=220–246|oclc=1122692088}}</ref>{{Page needed|date=June 2023}}

== Orbit ==
The orbit of a planet is the time and path it takes to go around the Sun. In the time it takes for Jupiter to orbit the Sun once, the Earth orbits the Sun 11.86 times.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://zebu.uoregon.edu/~soper/Orbits/jupiterorbit.html |title=Jupiter's path in the sky |accessdate=July 20, 2009 |format= |publisher=The Electronic Universe |archive-date=February 9, 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090209201346/https://zebu.uoregon.edu/~soper/Orbits/jupiterorbit.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> One [[year]] on Jupiter is equal to 11.86 years on Earth. 

The [[wikt:average|average]] distance between Jupiter and the Sun is 778 million [[kilometre]]s. This is five times the distance between Earth and the Sun. Jupiter is not tilted on its [[Cartesian coordinate system|axis]] as much as Earth or [[Mars]]. This causes it to have no seasons, for example [[summer]] or [[winter]]. Jupiter rotates, or spins around very quickly.<ref name=cain>{{cite web |url=https://www.universetoday.com/23914/rotation-of-jupiter/ |title=Rotation of Jupiter |first=Fraser |last=Cain |work=universetoday.com |date=January 20, 2009 |accessdate=April 1, 2015 |archive-date=May 26, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526124127/https://www.universetoday.com/23914/rotation-of-jupiter/ |url-status=live }}</ref> This causes the planet to [[wikt:bulge|bulge]] in the middle. Jupiter is the fastest spinning planet in the Solar System.<ref name=cain/> It completes one rotation or spin in 10 hours.<ref name=what /> Because of the bulge, the length of the [[equator]] of Jupiter is longer than the length from [[Geographical pole|pole]] to pole.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://ase.tufts.edu/cosmos/view_chapter.asp?id=9&page=3 |title=Jupiter: a giant primitive planet |accessdate=July 20, 2009 |format= |publisher=NASA's Cosmos |archive-date=May 14, 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110514093512/https://ase.tufts.edu/cosmos/view_chapter.asp?id=9&page=3 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

== Jupiter in the Solar System ==
=== Grand tack hypothesis ===
The orbit of Jupiter is unusual compared to planets in other star systems. It is usual for giant planets to be much nearer to their stars. Because Jupiter is not, this suggests an unusual explanation is needed for the arrangement of the planets in the Solar System. Astronomers have an idea on why this happened. It is called the [[grand tack hypothesis]].<ref name="Brasser_etal_2016a">{{cite journal|last1=Brasser|first1=R.|last2=Matsumura|first2=S.|last3=Ida|first3=S.|last4=Mojzsis|first4=S.J.|author5-link=Stephanie C. Werner| year=2016|title=Analysis of terrestrial planet formation by the Grand Tack model: System architecture and tack location.| journal=The Astrophysical Journal |volume=821|issue=2|pages=75|arxiv=1603.01009|bibcode=2016ApJ...821...75B|doi=10.3847/0004-637X/821/2/75|s2cid=119207767 |doi-access=free }}</ref><ref name="O'Brien_2014">{{cite journal|last1=O'Brien|first1=David P.|last2=Walsh|first2=Kevin J.|last3=Morbidelli|first3=Alessandro|last4=Raymond|first4=Sean N.|last5=Mandell|first5=Avi M.|date=2014|title=Water delivery and giant impacts in the 'Grand Tack' scenario|journal=Icarus|volume=239|pages=74–84|arxiv=1407.3290|bibcode=2014Icar..239...74O|doi=10.1016/j.icarus.2014.05.009|s2cid=51737711}}</ref>{{Page needed|date=June 2023}}<ref name="Matsumura_etal_2016">{{cite journal|last1=Matsumura|first1=Soko|last2=Brasser|first2=Ramon|last3=Ida|first3=Shigeru|date=2016|title=Effects of Dynamical Evolution of Giant Planets on the Delivery of Atmophile Elements during Terrestrial Planet Formation|journal=The Astrophysical Journal|volume=818|issue=1|page=15|arxiv=1512.08182|bibcode=2016ApJ...818...15M|doi=10.3847/0004-637X/818/1/15|s2cid=119205579 |doi-access=free }}</ref>

It is suggested that Jupiter formed about 3.5 [[astronomical unit]]s from the Sun. It started migrating inward and scattered the rocky planet-forming materials out beyond its orbit.<ref name="Fesenmaier_2015">{{cite web|last1=Fesenmaier|first1=Kimm|date=March 23, 2015|title=New research suggests Solar system may have once harbored super-Earths|url=https://www.caltech.edu/news/new-research-suggests-solar-system-may-have-once-harbored-super-earths-46017|access-date=November 5, 2015|publisher=[[Caltech]]}}</ref> Saturn formed later than Jupiter and started its own inward migration. When Jupiter reached 1.5 astronomical units, it became locked into an [[orbital resonance]] with Saturn.<ref name="Walsh_etal_2011">{{cite journal|last1=Walsh|first1=Kevin J.|last2=Morbidelli|first2=Alessandro|last3=Raymond|first3=Sean N.|last4=O'Brien|first4=David P.|last5=Mandell|first5=Avi M.|year=2011|title=A low mass for Mars from Jupiter's early gas-driven migration|url=https://archive.org/details/sim_nature-uk_2011-07-14_475_7355/page/206|journal=[[Nature (journal)|Nature]]|volume=475|issue=7355|pages=206–209|arxiv=1201.5177|bibcode=2011Natur.475..206W|doi=10.1038/nature10201|pmid=21642961|s2cid=4431823}}</ref> Both planets turned around and moved outward until Jupiter arrived at its current position, 5.2 astronomical units from the Sun. Saturn arrived at about 7 astronomical units.<ref name="Pierens_Raymond_2011">{{cite journal|last1=Pierens|first1=A.|last2=Raymond|first2=S.N.|date=2011|title=Two phase, inward-then-outward migration of Jupiter and Saturn in the gaseous solar nebula|journal=Astronomy & Astrophysics|volume=533|page=A131|arxiv=1107.5656|bibcode=2011A&A...533A.131P|doi=10.1051/0004-6361/201117451|doi-access=free |s2cid=67818537}}</ref>

The grand tack hypothesis explains another mystery of the Solar System. Mars should have been larger than Earth but is instead only {{Frac|1|10}} of this size. On Jupiter's grand tack, it cleared the area where Mars orbits today. After it left, the material remaining was only enough to form a small planet and a low-mass [[asteroid belt]].<ref name="Raynond_etal_2009">{{cite journal|last1=Raymond|first1=Sean N.|last2=O'Brien|first2=David P.|last3=Morbidelli|first3=Alessandro|last4=Kaib|first4=Nathan A.|year=2009|title=Building the terrestrial planets: Constrained accretion in the inner Solar System|journal=[[Icarus (journal)|Icarus]]|volume=203|issue=2|pages=644–662|arxiv=0905.3750|bibcode=2009Icar..203..644R|doi=10.1016/j.icarus.2009.05.016|s2cid=15578957}}</ref>{{Page needed|date=June 2023}}<ref>NASA. {{Cite web |url=https://www.nasa.gov/topics/solarsystem/features/young-jupiter.html |title=History of Jupiter |access-date=February 20, 2022 |archive-date=March 1, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170301170530/https://www.nasa.gov/topics/solarsystem/features/young-jupiter.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|title=Astrophysics of planet formation|last=Armitage|first=Philip J.|date=2020|isbn=978-1-108-34422-7|edition=2nd|location=Cambridge|pages=290–293|oclc=1122692088}}</ref> Although the hypothesis has not been absolutely proven, there is no other competing explanation why the Solar System's giant should be so far from its star, and Mars so small.

=== Asteroids and comets ===
[[File:InnerSolarSystem-en.png|thumb|A diagram of the inner Solar System. It shows the two groups of [[Trojan asteroid]]s that follow Jupiter in its orbit]]

Jupiter's large gravity has had an effect on the [[Solar System]]. Jupiter protects the inner planets from [[comet]]s by pulling them towards itself. Because of this, Jupiter has the most comet impacts in the Solar System.<ref name="Shoemaker-Levy" /> Jupiter has 95 known [[natural satellite]]s.<ref name="SheppardMoons">{{cite web|title= Moons of Jupiter|url= https://sites.google.com/carnegiescience.edu/sheppard/moons/jupitermoons|first= Scott S.|last= Sheppard|work= Earth & Planets Laboratory|publisher= Carnegie Institution for Science|accessdate= December 20, 2022|archive-date= April 24, 2019|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20190424041910/https://sites.google.com/carnegiescience.edu/sheppard/moons/jupitermoons|url-status= live}}</ref>

Two groups of [[asteroid]]s, called [[Trojan asteroid]]s, have settled into Jupiter's orbit around the Sun. One group is called the ''Trojans'' and the other group is called the ''Greeks''. They go around the Sun at the same time as Jupiter.<ref>{{cite journal|last=Wright|first=Alison|date=August 1, 2011|title=Planetary science: The Trojan is out there|url=https://www.nature.com/nphys/journal/v7/n8/full/nphys2061.html|url-status=live|journal=Nature Physics|volume=7|issue=8|page=592|bibcode=2011NatPh...7..592W|doi=10.1038/nphys2061|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200531220612/https://www.nature.com/articles/nphys2061|archive-date=May 31, 2020|accessdate=August 12, 2011}}</ref><ref>Robutel P. & Souchay J. 2010. "An introduction to the dynamics of trojan asteroids", in Dvorak, Rudolf & Souchay, Jean ''Dynamics of small Solar System bodies and exoplanets''. Lecture Notes in Physics, 790, Springer. p197 {{ISBN|3642044573}}</ref>

== Research and exploration ==
=== From Earth ===
Jupiter is the third brightest object in the night sky, after the Moon and [[Venus]].<ref name="unaidedeye">{{cite web|title=The biggest single object we can see in our galaxy, with the unaided eye|url=https://www.gsfc.nasa.gov/scienceques2001/20020201.htm|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090605113054/https://www.gsfc.nasa.gov/scienceques2001/20020201.htm|archive-date=June 5, 2009|publisher=NASA|format=|accessdate=September 26, 2013}}</ref> The first person known to really study the planet was [[Galileo Galilei]] in 1610.<ref name=Galileo /> He was the first person to see Jupiter's [[Natural satellite|moons]] Io, [[Europa (moon)|Europa]], [[Ganymede (moon)|Ganymede]] and [[Callisto]].<ref name=Galileo>{{cite web|url=https://www2.jpl.nasa.gov/galileo/ganymede/discovery.html|title=Jupiter|publisher=NASA|accessdate=July 17, 2009|archive-date=August 25, 2011|archive-url=https://www.webcitation.org/61Cw3Se9O?url=https://solarsystem.nasa.gov/galileo/|url-status=dead}}</ref> This was because he used a [[telescope]], unlike anyone before him.{{citation needed|date=May 2023}}

No new moons were discovered for more than two hundred years. In 1892, [[astronomer]] [[E.E Barnard|E.E. Barnard]] found a new moon using his [[observatory]] in [[California]]. He called the moon [[Amalthea]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.phys-astro.sonoma.edu/BruceMedalists/Barnard/ |title=Edward Emerson Barnard |accessdate=July 20, 2009 |format= |publisher=The Bruce Medalists |archive-date=September 17, 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110917023559/https://www.phys-astro.sonoma.edu/BruceMedalists/Barnard/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> It was the last of Jupiter's 67 moons to be discovered by [[human]] observation through a telescope.<ref name=Galileo />
In 1994, bits of the [[comet]] [[Shoemaker Levy-9]] hit Jupiter. It was the first time a collision between two [[Solar System]] objects was seen.<ref name="Shoemaker-Levy">{{cite web |url=https://www2.jpl.nasa.gov/sl9/ |title=Comet Shoemaker-Levy Collision with Jupiter |accessdate=July 20, 2009 |format= |publisher=NASA |archive-date=August 25, 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080825002013/https://www2.jpl.nasa.gov/sl9/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== From spacecraft ===
Seven spacecrafts have flown past Jupiter since 1973.<ref name=what /> These were [[Pioneer 10]] (1973), [[Pioneer 11]] (1974), Voyagers 1 and [[Voyager 2|2]] (1979), [[Ulysses (spacecraft)|Ulysses]] (1992 and 2004), [[Cassini-Huygens|Cassini]] (2000) and [[New Horizons]] (2007). Two spacecraft have been brought into orbit around Jupiter. These were Galileo (1995) and [[Juno (spacecraft)|Juno]] (2011).{{citation needed|date=May 2023}}

The Pioneer missions were the first spacecraft to take close-up pictures of Jupiter and its moons. Five years later, the two Voyager spacecraft discovered three new moons. They captured [[photo]] [[evidence]] of lightning on the night side of Jupiter.<ref>{{cite journal |url=https://adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1981JGR....86.8713G |title=The helium abundance of Jupiter from Voyager |accessdate=July 20, 2009 |publisher=Harvard University |bibcode=1981JGR....86.8713G |last1=Gautier |first1=D. |last2=Conrath |first2=B. |last3=Flasar |first3=M. |last4=Hanel |first4=R. |last5=Kunde |first5=V. |last6=Chedin |first6=A. |last7=Scott |first7=N. |journal=Journal of Geophysical Research |year=1981 |volume=86 |issue=A10 |page=8713 |doi=10.1029/JA086iA10p08713 |hdl=2060/19810016480 |s2cid=122314894 |archive-date=February 19, 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080219045637/https://adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1981JGR....86.8713G |url-status=live }}</ref>

The Ulysses probe was sent to study the Sun. It only went to Jupiter after it had finished its main mission. Ulysses had no [[camera]]s so it took no photographs.
In 2006, the Cassini spacecraft, on its way to [[Saturn (planet)|Saturn]], took some very good, very clear pictures of the planet. Cassini also found a moon and took a picture of it but it was too far away to show the details.<ref>{{cite journal |url=https://adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2004Icar..172....1H |title=The Cassini-Huygens flyby of Jupiter |accessdate=July 20, 2009 |format= |publisher=Harvard University |bibcode=2004Icar..172....1H |last1=Hansen |first1=Candice J. |last2=Bolton |first2=Scott J. |last3=Matson |first3=Dennis L. |last4=Spilker |first4=Linda J. |last5=Lebreton |first5=Jean-Pierre |journal=Icarus |year=2004 |volume=172 |issue=1 |page=1 |doi=10.1016/j.icarus.2004.06.018 |archive-date=January 15, 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080115122135/https://adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2004Icar..172....1H |url-status=live }}</ref>

The Galileo mission in 1995 was the first spacecraft to go into orbit around Jupiter. It flew around the planet for seven years and studied the four biggest moons. It launched a probe into the planet to get [[information]] about Jupiter's atmosphere. The probe travelled to a depth of about 150&nbsp;km before it was crushed by the pressure of all the [[gas]] above it.<ref name=Gal2>{{cite web |url=https://www2.jpl.nasa.gov/galileo/ |title=Galileo End of Mission Status |accessdate=July 20, 2009 |format= |publisher=NASA |archive-date=July 2, 2012 |archive-url=https://www.webcitation.org/68rIYpsgf?url=https://solarsystem.nasa.gov/galileo/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> The Galileo spacecraft was also crushed in 2003 when NASA [[steer]]ed the craft into the planet. They did this so that the craft could not crash into Europa, a [[Satellite (natural)|moon]] that scientists think might have [[life]].<ref name=Gal2 />

NASA has sent another spacecraft to Jupiter called Juno. It was launched on August 5, 2011<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nasa.gov/mission_pages/juno/launch/index.html|title=Juno Launch|publisher=NASA|accessdate=July 5, 2011|first=Tony|last=Greicius|date=May 24, 2011|archive-date=July 7, 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110707192438/https://www.nasa.gov/mission_pages/juno/launch/index.html|url-status=live}}</ref> and arrived at Jupiter on July 4, 2016.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nasa.gov/press-release/nasas-juno-spacecraft-in-orbit-around-mighty-jupiter|title=NASA's Juno Spacecraft in orbit around mighty Jupiter|publisher=NASA|date=July 5, 2016|accessdate=July 22, 2016|archive-date=July 8, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160708130530/https://www.nasa.gov/press-release/nasas-juno-spacecraft-in-orbit-around-mighty-jupiter/|url-status=live}}</ref> NASA published some results from the Juno mission in March 2018.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nasa.gov/feature/jpl/nasa-juno-findings-jupiter-s-jet-streams-are-unearthly|title=NASA Juno Findings - Jupiter's jet-streams are unearthly|publisher=NASA|date=March 7, 2018|accessdate=December 5, 2019|archive-date=January 10, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200110153427/https://www.nasa.gov/feature/jpl/nasa-juno-findings-jupiter-s-jet-streams-are-unearthly/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Several other missions have been planned to send spacecraft to Jupiter's moons, Europa, Callisto, and Ganymede. One called JIMO (Jupiter Icy Moons Orbiter) was cancelled in 2006 because it cost too much [[money]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.msnbc.msn.com/id/6928404/ |title=White House scales back space plans |work=msnbc.com |date=7 February 2005 |accessdate=July 20, 2009 |format= |publisher=MSN |archive-date=August 22, 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110822025509/https://www.msnbc.msn.com/id/6928404/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The [[European Space Agency]] launched [[Jupiter Icy Moons Explorer|JUICE (Jupiter Icy Moons Explorer)]] on April 14, 2023. It will enter orbit around Jupiter in July 2031.<ref name="bbc-20230214">{{Cite news|date=April 14, 2023|title=European Space Agency: Blast off for Jupiter icy moons mission|language=en-GB|work=BBC News|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/science-environment-65273857|access-date=April 14, 2023}}</ref>

== Moons ==
{{main|List of Jupiter's moons}}

Jupiter has 115 known moons, as of April 9, 2026.<ref name="JPL_moon_count" /><ref>{{Cite web|last=Roberts|first=Glenn|date=March 16, 2023|title=ATLANTIC SKIES: All in the family — the number of moons orbiting around Jupiter keeps growing and growing|url=https://www.saltwire.com/atlantic-canada/lifestyles/atlantic-skies-all-in-the-family-the-number-of-moons-orbiting-around-jupiter-keeps-growing-and-growing-100832545/|website=www.saltwire.com}}</ref> The four largest were seen by [[Galileo]] with his primitive telescope, and thus are known as the [[Galilean moons]], and nine more can be seen with modern telescopes. Three moons were identified by the Voyager spacecraft. All other moons were first seen on Earth, using modern telescopes and advanced photography methods.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov/append7.html#JovianSystem|title=Jovian System|publisher=U.S. Geological Survey|accessdate=July 24, 2009|archive-date=August 25, 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090825153846/https://planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov/append7.html#JovianSystem|url-status=live}}</ref> The smallest moon ([[S/2003 J 12]]) is only one [[kilometre]] across. The largest, Ganymede, has a diameter of 5,262 kilometres. It is bigger than the planet [[Mercury (planet)|Mercury]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nineplanets.org/ganymede.html |title=Ganymede |accessdate=July 20, 2009 |format= |publisher=Nine Planets |archive-date=February 4, 2012 |archive-url=https://www.webcitation.org/65CgnurcG?url=https://nineplanets.org/ganymede.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The other three Galilean moons are Io, Europa and Callisto. Due to the way they orbit Jupiter, gravity affects three of these moons greatly. The [[friction]] caused by the gravity of Europa and Ganymede pulling on Io makes it the most [[volcanic]] object in the Solar System. It has over 400 volcanoes, more than three times as many as Earth.<ref>{{cite journal |url=https://adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2004Icar..169..140L |title=Lava lakes on Io: observations of Io's volcanic activity from Galileo NIMS during the 2001 fly-bys |accessdate=July 20, 2009 |format= |publisher=Harvard University |bibcode=2004Icar..169..140L |last1=Lopes |first1=Rosaly M. C. |last2=Kamp |first2=Lucas W. |last3=Smythe |first3=William D. |last4=Mouginis-Mark |first4=Peter |last5=Kargel |first5=Jeff |last6=Radebaugh |first6=Jani |last7=Turtle |first7=Elizabeth P. |last8=Perry |first8=Jason |last9=Williams |first9=David A. |last10=Carlson |first10=R. W. |last11=Douté |first11=S. |author12=the Galileo NIMS |author13=SSI Teams |journal=Icarus |year=2004 |volume=169 |issue=1 |page=140 |doi=10.1016/j.icarus.2003.11.013 |archive-date=September 17, 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070917190921/https://adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2004Icar..169..140L |url-status=live }}</ref>

