{{about|the [[planet]]|other uses|Venus (disambiguation)}}

'''Venus''' is the second [[planet]] from the [[Sun]].<ref name=":2">{{Cite web|title=Venus|url=https://solarsystem.nasa.gov/planets/venus/overview|access-date=October 19, 2021|website=NASA Solar System Exploration|archive-date=October 19, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211019035311/https://solarsystem.nasa.gov/planets/venus/overview/|url-status=live}}</ref> Venus is the only planet in the Solar System that has a day longer than a year. The year length of Venus is 225 Earth days. The day length of Venus is 243 Earth days.<ref name=":2" />
==Overview==
Venus is a ''[[terrestrial planet]]'' because it has a solid, [[Rock (geology)|rocky]] surface like other planets in the inner [[Solar System]]. [[Astronomer|Astronomers]] have known Venus for [[Millennia|thousands of years]]. The [[Ancient Rome|ancient Romans]] named it after their [[Deity|goddess]] [[Venus (mythology)|Venus]], goddess of [[love]] and [[beauty]].<ref name=":2" />

Venus is the brightest thing in the night sky except for the [[Moon]]. It is sometimes called the ''morning star'' or the ''evening star'' as at some [[Elongation|elongations]] it is easily seen just before the [[Sun]] comes up in the morning. At other times, it can be seen just after the sun goes down in the [[evening]]. Venus comes closer to the [[Earth]] than any other [[planet]] does.
===Composition===
Venus is sometimes called the [[sister]] planet of Earth as they are quite similar in [[size]] and [[gravity]]. In other ways the planets are very different. Venus' [[atmosphere]] is mostly [[carbon dioxide]] (CO<sub>2</sub>) with clouds of [[Sulfuric acid|sulphuric acid]] (H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>).<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.windows.ucar.edu/tour/link=/venus/atmosphere.html|title=The Atmosphere of Venus|access-date=August 19, 2008|archive-date=March 2, 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090302062611/http://www.windows.ucar.edu/tour/link=/venus/atmosphere.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> is a [[chemical]] that is [[poison]]ous to life. For this it is sometimes known as the Earth's "evil twin".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hall |first1=Shannon |title=Venus is Earth's evil twin — and space agencies can no longer resist its pull |url=https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-019-01730-5 |accessdate=June 6, 2021 |work=Nature |date=June 5, 2019 |archive-date=June 5, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210605192557/https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-019-01730-5 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Greenfieldboyce |first1=Nell |title=NASA Picks Twin Missions To Visit Venus, Earth's 'Evil Twin' |url=https://www.npr.org/2021/06/02/1002627399/nasa-picks-twin-missions-to-visit-venus-earths-evil-twin |accessdate=June 6, 2021 |work=NPR |date=June 2, 2021 |archive-date=June 5, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210605231100/https://www.npr.org/2021/06/02/1002627399/nasa-picks-twin-missions-to-visit-venus-earths-evil-twin |url-status=live }}</ref>

The thick atmosphere makes it hard to see the surface. Until the late twentieth century many thought there might be life there. The [[pressure]] on Venus' surface is 92 times that of Earth. Venus is one of only two planets in the Solar System (the other being [[Mercury (planet)|Mercury]]) that has no [[Satellite (natural)|moons]].  Venus spins very slowly on its axis and it spins in the [[Retrograde and direct motion|opposite direction]] to the other planets.

== Physical properties ==
[[File:Venus globe.jpg|thumb|[[Radar]] view of the surface of Venus ([[Magellan spacecraft]])]]

Venus is a terrestrial planet so, like the Earth, its surface is made of rock. Venus is much hotter than Earth. All the [[carbon dioxide]] in the atmosphere acts like a [[blanket]], trapping heat from the Sun. This effect is called the [[greenhouse effect]] and it is very strong on Venus. This makes the surface of Venus the hottest of any planet's surface in the Solar System with an [[estimate]]d [[average]] [[temperature]] of {{convert|753.1|K|C+F}}.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://filer.case.edu/sjr16/advanced/venus.html|title=Venus - an overview|access-date=August 20, 2008|archive-date=September 11, 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090911091043/http://filer.case.edu/sjr16/advanced/venus.html|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://hypertextbook.com/facts/2000/GeorgeRyabov.shtml|title=Temperature on the Surface of Venus|access-date=August 19, 2008|archive-date=September 28, 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080928112636/http://hypertextbook.com/facts/2000/GeorgeRyabov.shtml|url-status=live}}</ref> This is hot enough to [[melting|melt]] [[lead]] or [[zinc]].

=== Geography ===
Venus has no oceans because it is much too hot for water. Venus' surface is a dry desert. Because of the clouds, only [[radar]] can map the surface. It is about 80% smooth, [[Rock (geology)|rocky]] [[plain]]s, made mostly of [[basalt]]. Two higher areas called [[continent]]s make up the north and south of the planet. The north is called ''Ishtar Terra'' and the south is called ''Aphrodite Terra''. They are named after the [[Babylon]]ian and [[Greek god]]desses of love.<ref>Batson R.M. & Russell J.F. 1991. ''Naming the newly found landforms on Venus''. ''Abstracts of the Lunar and Planetary Science Conference'', v. 22, p65.</ref>

The surface of Venus looks like it has been shaped by [[Volcanism|volcanic activity]]. Venus has a lot of [[volcano]]es.<ref name=":0">{{Cite book|title=Volcanoes of the solar system|last=Frankel, Charles.|date=1996|publisher=Cambridge University Press|isbn=0-521-47201-6|location=Cambridge|oclc=32969544}}</ref> The surface of Venus is estimated to be 300–600 million years old.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Nimmo|first1=F.|last2=Mckenzie|first2=D.|title=Volcanism and Tectonics on Venus|journal=Annual Review of Earth and Planetary Sciences|date=1998|volume=26|pages=23–51|doi=10.1146/ANNUREV.EARTH.26.1.23|bibcode=1998AREPS..26...23N|s2cid=862354| issn = 0084-6597 }}</ref>

Unlike [[Earth]] or [[Mars]], Venus does not have defined highlands or lowlands, and it does not have [[tectonic plates]].

=== Atmosphere ===

Venus' [[atmosphere]] is mostly [[carbon dioxide]] and [[nitrogen]] [[gas]] with [[cloud]]s of [[sulphuric acid]]. Because the atmosphere is so thick or dense the [[pressure]] is very high. The pressure is 92 times the pressure on Earth, enough to crush many things.

It is impossible to see the planet's surface from [[Outer space|space]] as the thick [[cloud]] layer [[reflection|reflects]] 60% of the light that hits it. The only way scientists are able to see it is by using [[infrared]] and [[UV|ultraviolet]] [[camera]]s and [[radar]].

Scientists believe that billions of years ago, the atmosphere of Venus could have been like Earth's atmosphere. There may have been lots of water on the surface of Venus. But after 600 million to several billion years, the [[evaporation]] of the water put [[greenhouse gas]]es into its atmosphere.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Kasting|first=James F.|date=1988|title=Runaway and moist greenhouse atmospheres and the evolution of Earth and Venus|url=https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/0019103588901169|journal=Icarus|language=en|volume=74|issue=3|pages=472–494|doi=10.1016/0019-1035(88)90116-9|pmid=11538226|bibcode=1988Icar...74..472K|via=|access-date=September 14, 2020|archive-date=May 31, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200531163723/https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0019103588901169?via%3Dihub|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Magnetic field===
In 1967, [[Venera 4]] found that the magnetic field of Venus was much weaker than that of Earth. This magnetic field is induced by an interaction between the [[ionosphere]] and the [[solar wind]]. Venus' magnetosphere is not strong enough to protect the atmosphere from [[cosmic ray]]s.<ref>{{Cite book|title=Introduction to space physics|url=https://archive.org/details/introductiontosp0000unse_w5p3|date=1995|publisher=Cambridge University Press|others=Kivelson, M. G. (Margaret Galland), 1928-, Russell, C. T. (Christopher T.)|isbn=0-521-45104-3|location=Cambridge|oclc=30509600}}</ref>

===Transit of Venus===
{{main|Transit of Venus}}
Venus can sometimes be seen passing between the Sun and Earth. Venus looks like a black dot when seen through a special telescope. These passages are called "transits". These "transits" happen in pairs eight years apart. Then it is more than a hundred years to the next pair.

==Orbit and rotation==
{{Main|Orbit of Venus}}
[[File:Venusorbitsolarsystem.gif|thumb|right|Orbit of Venus compared to the orbit of the Earth]]Venus orbits the Sun at an average [[distance]] of about 108 million km (68~ million mi). It completes an orbit every 224.7 days.<ref>{{Cite web|date=March 4, 2016|title=Venus Fact Sheet|url=http://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/venusfact.html|access-date=September 14, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304130722/http://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/venusfact.html|archive-date=March 4, 2016}}</ref> The rotation of Venus is slow. A Venusian [[sidereal day]] is “..NOT..” longer than a Venusian year.::.

