The '''European Union''' ([[abbreviation]]: '''EU''') is a [[union]] of 27 member [[country|countries]] in [[Europe]] on the  landmass of [[Eurasia]]. It was created by the [[Treaty of Maastricht|Maastricht Treaty]] in 1993. It is a unique [[Politics|political]] [[organization]]; in some ways, it acts like a [[federation]], and in other ways, it acts like a [[confederation]]. (Political scientists call the EU a "[[sui generis]]" organization, which means the EU is so unique that it is hard to compare it to anything else.)

The EU grew out of the '''European Economic Community''' ('''EEC'''), which was formed by the [[Treaty of Rome]] in 1957. It has created a common [[economic area]] with Europe-wide [[Law|laws]], allowing the [[Citizenship|citizens]] of [[EU ]] countries to move and [[trade]] in other EU countries almost the same as they do in their own. Nineteen of these countries also share the same type of [[money]]: the [[euro]].

The [[Treaty of Lisbon]] is the most recent [[treaty]] that explains how the Union is run. Every member [[state]] signed it, to show that they each agreed with what it says. Most importantly, it says which jobs (’[[Power|powers]]’) the Union should do for the members and which jobs they should do themselves. The members decide how the Union should act by voting for or against proposals.

The objective of the [[European Union]] is to bring its member states closer together, while also respecting [[human rights]], [[democracy]] and [[rule of law]]. It does this by helping its members to decide on common rules for many things, like [[law enforcement]],  [[passport|passports]], and how to trade fairly with each other.

Most members share a common currency (the euro) and most allow people to travel from one country to another without having to show a passport.

== History ==
[[File:Quai d'Orsay.jpg|thumbnail|Quai d'Orsay (Paris). Robert Schuman gave the speech starting the plan for a European Coal and Steel Community in 1950.]]
After [[World War II]], most countries in Europe wanted to live peacefully together and help one another's [[economy|economies]]. Instead of fighting each other for [[coal]] and [[steel]], the first member countries-- [[Germany|(West) Germany]], [[France]], [[Italy]], [[Belgium]], [[Netherlands]], and [[Luxembourg]]--  created the [[European Coal and Steel Community]] in [[1952]]. Its job was to make rules for how to trade coal and steel in a fair way, and to make sure its members were following the rules.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2016-08-07|title=The European Communities|url=https://www.cvce.eu/obj/the_european_communities-en-3940ef1d-7c10-4d0f-97fc-0cf1e86a32d4.html|access-date=2025-03-15|website=CVCE.EU by UNI.LU|language=en}}</ref>

Since 1974, in the Cypriot city of [[Nicosia]], is considered the last divided capital in europe.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Treaty of Rome|url=https://www.europarl.europa.eu/about-parliament/en/in-the-past/the-parliament-and-the-treaties/treaty-of-rome|access-date=2025-03-15|website=Treaty of Rome|language=en}}</ref> 

In 1993, with the ''[[Treaty of Maastricht]]'', the ECSC joined together with a few other international organizations in Europe to form the '''European Union.''' Now it would help its member countries work together in many different ways; not only with trade, but also with money, justice ([[law]]s), and [[Foreign policy|foreign affairs]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Maastricht Treaty|url=https://www.europarl.europa.eu/about-parliament/en/in-the-past/the-parliament-and-the-treaties/maastricht-treaty|access-date=2025-03-15|website=Maastricht Treaty|language=en}}</ref> 

In 1995, the EU created the [[Schengen Area]], and 22 member countries of the EU opened their [[Border|borders]] to each other. This means that people can now travel from one country to the other without a [[passport]] or [[identity card]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Understanding the Schengen Agreement: History, Purpose, and Impacts|url=https://etias.com/articles/schengen-agreement|access-date=2025-03-15|website=etias.com|language=en}}</ref> 

In 1999, the EU created its own [[currency]], which is called the [[euro]]. Many of its member states got rid of their old currencies, and now use the euro. However, a few countries, like Denmark and Poland, chose to keep their own currency.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Countries using the euro {{!}} European Union|url=https://european-union.europa.eu/institutions-law-budget/euro/countries-using-euro_en|access-date=2025-03-15|website=european-union.europa.eu|language=en}}</ref>

Over time, more countries have joined the EU. In 2004, ten new countries became part of the EU: Cyprus, Czechia, Estonia, Hungary, Latvia, Lithuania, Malta, Poland, Slovakia, and Slovenia.  In 2007, two more countries, Bulgaria and Romania, joined the EU. Most recently, Croatia joined the EU in 2013.<ref>{{Cite web|title=EU enlargement - European Union|url=https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-enlargement_en|access-date=2025-03-15|website=european-union.europa.eu|language=en}}</ref> Then, in 2020, the [[Brexit|United Kingdom left the EU]].<ref>{{Cite news|date=2015-05-21|title=Brexit: What you need to know about the UK leaving the EU|language=en-GB|work=BBC News|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-politics-32810887|access-date=2025-03-15}}</ref> As of 2025, there are 27 member countries.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Key facts and figures {{!}} European Union|url=https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/facts-and-figures-european-union_en|access-date=2025-03-15|website=european-union.europa.eu|language=en}}</ref>

The EU is now the world's largest single market, with a population of about 450 million people.<ref name="facts">{{cite web|url=https://www.statista.com/topics/921/european-union/#statisticChapter|title=The European Union - Statistics & Facts|publisher=Statista|accessdate=17 February 2025}}</ref> If the EU were a single country, it would be the world's third largest country.<ref name="facts" />

In [[Tariffs in the second Trump administration|2025 there were United States tariffs against the European Union]]. In April, the EU informed that from April 15, there would be 25% tariffs on some U.S. goods.<ref>https://e24.no/boers-og-finans/i/PpAaOX/eu-banker-gjennom-tollsvar-mot-usa?referer=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.vg.no. Retrieved 2025-04-09</ref> The situation is sometimes called a [[trade war]].

== Free movement ==
A person who is a [[citizen]] of the European Union can live and work in any of the 27 member states without needing a [[work permit]] or [[visa]]. For example, a [[French people|French]] person can move to [[Greece]] to work there, or just to live there, and he or she does not need permission from an [[authority]] in Greece.

[[Product (business)|Products]] made in one member country can be sold in any other member country without any special permissions or extra [[Tax|taxes]]. For this reason, the members agree to rules about product [[safety]] - they want to know that a product made in another country will be as safe as it would be if it had been made in their own.
