== Brexit ==
On June 23, 2016, the UK held a [[referendum]] on whether it should stay in the EU or leave it. The majority [52% to 48%] favored leaving.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-politics-32810887|title=Brexit: All you need to know about the UK leaving the EU|first=|last=|work=BBC News |date=30 December 2020 |publisher=|accessdate=}}</ref> Britain leaving the EU is commonly known as [[United Kingdom European Union membership referendum|Brexit]].

The government of the UK [[wikt:invoke|invoke]]d "Article 50" of the Treaty of European Union (the Treaty of Lisbon) on 29 March 2017.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-politics-39431428|title='No turning back' on Brexit as Article 50 triggered|author=Laura Kuenssberg|work=[[BBC News]]|accessdate=29 March 2017}}</ref> This began negotiations with fellow members of the EU on the terms of exit. The timetable for these negotiations was two years, which meant that the UK would remain a member of the EU until at least March 2019. This deadline was later extended to October 31st 2019 at the request of the British Government. The [[United Kingdom]] left the European Union on the 31 of January 2020 at 23:00 ([[Greenwich Mean Time]]). Following [[Brexit|that]], the '''transition period'''  expired on December 31, 2020 and ended on January 1, 2021.

== Foreign relations ==
*[[China–European Union relations]]. The EU is China's largest trading partner, and China is the EU's largest trade partner. "European Union ... agreed [in 2025] to [... put in place] a three-euro duty on low-value imports into [... EU] from July 2026", according to media; That is supposed "to help [against or] tackle a" [[trade imbalance]]].<ref>{{Cite news|last=AFP|date=2025-12-13|title=EU agrees three-euro small parcel tax to tackle China flood|language=en|work=Bangkok Post|url=https://www.bangkokpost.com/business/general/3155440/eu-agrees-threeeuro-small-parcel-tax-to-tackle-china-flood|access-date=2026-01-13}}</ref>

