[[File:Great Lakes Lake Michigan.png|thumb|200px|right|Map of the Great lakes, dark blue: Lake Michigan]]
'''Lake Michigan''' is one of the five [[Great Lakes]] in [[North America]]. It has a [[surface area]] of {{convert|22300|sqmi|km2|abbr=on}}. It is {{convert|267|by|103|NM|mi km}} wide, with an average depth of {{convert|46.5|fathom|ft m}} and a maximum depth of {{convert|154.2|fathom|ft m}}. Lake Michigan is the 5th-largest lake in the world. The lake is separated from, but connected to the [[Canada–United States border]]-shared [[Lake Huron]] by the {{convert|4.3|NM|mi km|adj=mid|-wide}}, {{convert|20|fathom|ft m|spell=in|adj=mid|-deep}} Straits of Mackinac.

Lake Michigan is surrounded by the [[U.S. state]]s of [[Indiana]], [[Illinois]], [[Wisconsin]], and [[Michigan]]. It is the only Great Lake that is not partly in [[Canada]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.great-lakes.net/lakes/michigan.html#overview|title=Lake Michigan|publisher=Great Lakes.net|accessdate=September 1, 2014|archive-date=January 1, 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100101170911/http://www.great-lakes.net/lakes/michigan.html#overview|url-status=dead}}</ref> The largest city on the shore of Lake Michigan is [[Chicago|Chicago, Illinois]].

The earliest time that people lived near the lake was 800 CE. The word "Michigan" originally meant the [[lake]] itself. It is believed to come from the [[Ojibwe language|Ojibwa]] word ''mishigami'' meaning "great water".<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.superiorwatersheds.org/projects.php?id=6|title=Superior Watershed Partnership Projects|access-date=2014-09-01|archive-date=2007-09-28|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070928221658/http://www.superiorwatersheds.org/projects.php?id=6|url-status=dead}}</ref>

The first person to reach the deepest part of Lake Michigan was [[J. Val Klump]]. Klump reached the bottom with a [[submarine]] as part of a 1985 research [[expedition]].<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Klump |first1=J. Val |last2=Paddock |first2=Robert |last3=Remsen |first3=Charles C. |last4=Fitzgerald |first4=Sharon |last5=Boraas |first5=Martin |last6=Anderson |first6=Patrick |title=Variations In Sediment Accumulation Rates And The Flux Of Labile Organic Matter In Eastern Lake Superior Basins |year=1989 |journal=[[The Journal of Great Lakes Research]] |volume=15 |issue=1 |page=104 |doi=10.1016/S0380-1330(89)71465-9 |url=http://loracsevents.com/dev/iaglr/dev/jglr/db/view_contents.php?pub_id=965&mode=view&table=yes&topic_id=30&mode=topic_section&volume=15&issue=1 |accessdate=2009-08-09 |bibcode=1989JGLR...15..104K |archive-date=2012-12-03 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20121203024023/http://loracsevents.com/dev/iaglr/dev/jglr/db/view_contents.php?pub_id=965&mode=view&table=yes&topic_id=30&mode=topic_section&volume=15&issue=1 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Twelve million people live along Lake Michigan's shores. They are mainly in the [[Chicago]] and [[Milwaukee]] areas.

