[[File:Gas particle movement.svg|thumb|right|200px|An illustration of the random way gas molecules move, without being attached to each other.]]

A '''gas''' is one of the main forms, or states, of [[matter]], like [[solid]]s, [[liquid]]s, and [[plasma]]s. What makes gases special is that they do not have a fixed shape or a set volume. Instead, gas particles, tiny [[atom]]s or [[molecule]]s, are spread out, move freely, and have a lot of [[energy]]. Because the particles are so far apart and do not stick to each other much, gases can spread out to fill any space or [[container]] they are in. Gases can also be squeezed into smaller spaces, which is called [[Compression (physical)|compression]]. Gases are really important in [[science]]. They help us understand how [[heat]], [[pressure]], and [[motion]] work. Gases are all around us, from the air we breathe to the [[fuel]] in [[engine]]s, and they play big roles in nature and technology.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2025-06-27|title=Gas {{!}} Definition, State of Matter, Properties, Structure, & Facts {{!}} Britannica|url=https://www.britannica.com/science/gas-state-of-matter|access-date=2025-07-02|website=www.britannica.com|language=en}}</ref>

Gases have special physical properties that make them different from solids and liquids. For example, gases can be easily [[Physical compression|compressed]] (squeezed into a smaller space), they have low [[density]] (they are much lighter than the same volume of solid or liquid), and they can spread out and mix with other gases easily (this is called [[diffusion]]). Gases also push against the walls of whatever container they are in. This push is called pressure and comes from gas particles bouncing around and hitting the container’s walls.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2013-10-03|title=Properties of Gas|url=https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry_Textbook_Maps/Supplemental_Modules_(Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry)/Physical_Properties_of_Matter/States_of_Matter/Properties_of_Gases/Properties_of_Gas|access-date=2025-07-02|website=Chemistry LibreTexts|language=en}}</ref> Scientists use a formula called the [[ideal gas law]] to describe how gases behave. It is written as PV = nRT, where:
* P is [[pressure]],
* V is [[volume]],
* n is the amount of gas (in [[Mole (unit)|mole]]s),
* R is a constant,
* T is [[temperature]].

This formula helps predict how a gas will act if you change its [[temperature]], [[pressure]], or [[volume]].<ref>{{Cite web|date=2013-10-02|title=The Ideal Gas Law|url=https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry_Textbook_Maps/Supplemental_Modules_(Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry)/Physical_Properties_of_Matter/States_of_Matter/Properties_of_Gases/Gas_Laws/The_Ideal_Gas_Law|access-date=2025-07-02|website=Chemistry LibreTexts|language=en}}</ref> But it is not perfect. [[Real gas]]es do not always follow this rule, especially when they are under very high pressure or low temperature. In those cases, the gas particles get closer together and start to interact, which changes how they behave. Scientists use more detailed equations, like the [[van der Waals equation]], to describe real gas behavior more accurately.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2014-11-18|title=10.9: Real Gases - Deviations from Ideal Behavior|url=https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Map%3A_Chemistry_-_The_Central_Science_(Brown_et_al.)/10%3A_Gases/10.09%3A_Real_Gases_-_Deviations_from_Ideal_Behavior|access-date=2025-07-02|website=Chemistry LibreTexts|language=en}}</ref>

Gases can change into other forms of matter, like liquids or solids, when the temperature or pressure changes. For example, when a gas cools down or gets squeezed tightly (compressed), it can turn into a liquid in a process called [[condensation]]. If the conditions are right, a gas can even turn directly into a solid without becoming a liquid first. This is called deposition. The opposite can also happen. If you heat a [[liquid]], it can become a gas through [[vaporization]] (like boiling water turning into [[steam]]). And sometimes, if you heat a solid, it can turn straight into a gas without melting first. This process is called [[sublimation]] (like dry ice turning into [[Carbon dioxide|carbon dioxide gas]]). These changes, called phase transitions, are very important. They happen all around us, in cloud formation, [[breathing]], and even during [[volcanic eruption]]s. They are also used in many industries, such as [[refrigeration]], distilling liquids, and making chemicals.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Condensation|url=https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/condensation|access-date=2025-07-02|website=education.nationalgeographic.org|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=2016-03-11|title=11.5: Vaporization and Vapor Pressure|url=https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Map%3A_A_Molecular_Approach_(Tro)/11%3A_Liquids_Solids_and_Intermolecular_Forces/11.05%3A_Vaporization_and_Vapor_Pressure|access-date=2025-07-02|website=Chemistry LibreTexts|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=2019-09-08|title=Sublimation and the Water Cycle {{!}} U.S. Geological Survey|url=https://www.usgs.gov/special-topics/water-science-school/science/sublimation-and-water-cycle|access-date=2025-07-02|website=www.usgs.gov|language=en}}</ref>

Gases can be made of just one kind of element or of different elements combined. Some gases are elemental, meaning they are made of only one type of [[atom]]. Examples include [[Hydrogen|hydrogen (H₂)]], [[Oxygen|oxygen (O₂)]], and the [[noble gas]]es like [[helium]] and [[neon]]. Other gases are compounds, which means they are made of two or more different atoms joined together. Examples of these include [[Carbon dioxide|carbon dioxide (CO₂)]], [[Methane|methane (CH₄)]], and [[Ammonia|ammonia (NH₃)]]. Gases can also be mixtures, where many gases are blended together. A good example is air, which we breathe every day. Air is mostly made of [[nitrogen]] and [[oxygen]], but it also contains small amounts of other gases like [[argon]] and carbon dioxide. These gas mixtures are super important. The mix of gases in Earth’s [[Atmosphere of Earth|atmosphere]] helps regulate the planet’s temperature and makes breathing possible. In factories and hospitals, special gas mixtures are used for [[welding]], [[surgery]], and even to keep food fresh.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2014-11-20|title=Gaseous Elements and Compounds|url=https://chem.libretexts.org/Courses/Howard_University/General_Chemistry%3A_An_Atoms_First_Approach/Unit_4%3A__Thermochemistry/Chapter_10%3A_Gases/Chapter_10.1%3A_Gaseous_Elements_and_Compounds|access-date=2025-07-02|website=Chemistry 003|language=en}}</ref>

Some gases belong to special groups because of how they behave or affect the world around us. Noble gases are a group of gases like helium, neon, and argon that do not easily react with other substances. This is because their atoms have a “full” outer shell of electrons, which makes them chemically stable or inert. That means they usually do not join with other atoms to form compounds.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2015-05-18|title=Group 18: Properties of Nobel Gases|url=https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Inorganic_Chemistry/Supplemental_Modules_and_Websites_(Inorganic_Chemistry)/Descriptive_Chemistry/Elements_Organized_by_Block/2_p-Block_Elements/Group_18%3A_The_Noble_Gases/1Group_18%3A_Properties_of_Nobel_Gases|access-date=2025-07-02|website=Chemistry LibreTexts|language=en}}</ref> [[Greenhouse gas]]es, like carbon dioxide (CO₂) and methane (CH₄), are gases that trap heat in Earth’s atmosphere. They let [[sunlight]] in but stop some of the [[heat]] from escaping, like a blanket. This helps keep the planet warm, but too much of these gases leads to [[global warming]] and [[climate change]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=US EPA|first=OAR|date=2015-12-23|title=Overview of Greenhouse Gases|url=https://www.epa.gov/ghgemissions/overview-greenhouse-gases|access-date=2025-07-02|website=www.epa.gov|language=en}}</ref> There are also toxic gases, which can make people sick or be dangerous to breathe, and flammable gases, which can catch [[fire]] easily. These types of gases must be handled carefully to avoid accidents.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Toxic and Hazardous Gas Classifications Chart - Institutional Risk & Safety {{!}} The University of Texas at Dallas|url=https://risk-safety.utdallas.edu/research-safety-programs/chemical-safety/compressed-gas/classifications-chart/|access-date=2025-07-02|language=en-US}}</ref> To study and work with gases, scientists use special tools like [[gas chromatography]] and [[mass spectrometry]]. These tools help them identify what gases are present, measure how much there is, and even separate or analyze them in great detail.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2013-10-02|title=Gas Chromatography|url=https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Analytical_Chemistry/Supplemental_Modules_(Analytical_Chemistry)/Instrumentation_and_Analysis/Chromatography/Gas_Chromatography|access-date=2025-07-02|website=Chemistry LibreTexts|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Citation|last1=Garg|first1=Eshita|title=Mass Spectrometer|date=2025|url=http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK589702/|work=StatPearls|place=Treasure Island (FL)|publisher=StatPearls Publishing|pmid=36944006|access-date=2025-07-02|last2=Zubair|first2=Muhammad}}</ref>

==Introduction==
In a pure gas, each molecule may be made of an individual [[atom]]. It may be elemental, where each [[molecule]] is made of more than one of the same atom [[Nuclear binding energy|bound together]]. It may be [[Chemical compound|compounds]] where molecules are made of many types of atoms together. An example of a monoatomic gas is [[neon]], an example of an elemental gas is [[hydrogen]] and an example of a compound gas is [[carbon dioxide]].{{cn|date=December 2024}}

==Examples==
===Composition===
A gas [[mixture]] contains a mix of any of the above types. [[air]] comprises 78% [[nitrogen]], 21% [[oxygen]], less than 1% [[argon]], around 0.03% [[carbon dioxide]] and more other in very small quantities.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Composition of Air|url=http://mistupid.com/chemistry/aircomp.htm|access-date=2024-01-18|website=mistupid.com}}</ref>{{better source|date=December 2024}}

==Applications==
===War===
[[Poison gas|Poison gases]] were used as [[Chemical weapon|chemical weapons]] in [[World War II|WWI]].{{cn|date=December 2024}}

==Characteristics==
All gases can [[Flux|flow]], like liquids. This means the molecules move about independently of each other. Most gases are colourless, like hydrogen.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.newton.dep.anl.gov/askasci/chem03/chem03787.htm|title=Colours of Gases|access-date=2010-01-09|archive-date=2009-10-19|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091019055802/http://www.newton.dep.anl.gov/askasci/chem03/chem03787.htm|url-status=dead}}</ref> Gas particles will spread about, or [[diffuse]], in order to fill all the space in any container such as a bottle or a room. Compared to liquids and solids, gases have a very low [[density]] and [[viscosity]]. We cannot directly see most gases since they aren't coloured. However it is possible to measure their density, [[volume]], temperature and [[pressure]].{{cn|date=December 2024}}
===Pressure===
{{main|Pressure}}
Pressure is the measure of how much pushing [[Force (physics)|force]] something is putting on another object. In a gas, this is usually the gas pushing on the container of the object or, if the gas is heavy, something inside the gas. Pressure is measured in [[Pascal (unit)|pascals]]. Because of [[Newton's third law]], we can change the pressure of a gas by putting force on the object containing it. For example, squeezing a bottle with air inside pressurises the air inside.{{cn|date=December 2024}}

When talking about gas, pressure is often related to the container. A lot of gas in a small container would have very high pressure. A small amount of a gas in a big container would have low pressure.
Gas can create pressure itself when there is a lot of it. The [[weight]] of the gas creates pressure on anything underneath it, including other gas. On a [[planet]], this is called [[atmospheric pressure]].{{cn|date=December 2024}}

===Temperature===
{{main|temperature}}
The temperature of a gas is how hot or cold it is. In [[Physica|physics]] it is usually measured in [[Kelvin]] although degrees [[Celsius]] are used more elsewhere. In a gas, the average velocity, i.e. how fast they move, of the molecules is related to the temperature. The faster the gas molecules are moving, the more they collide, or smash into each other. These [[Brownian motion|collisions]] release [[Energy#Scientific energy|energy]], which in a gas comes in the form of [[heat]]. Conversely if the temperature around the gas becomes hotter then the gas particles will convert the [[thermal energy]] to [[kinetic energy]], making them move faster and making the gas hotter.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://id.mind.net/~zona/mstm/physics/mechanics/energy/heatAndTemperature/heatAndTemperature.html|title=Heat and temperature|access-date=2010-01-10|archive-date=2010-02-10|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100210203551/http://id.mind.net/~zona/mstm/physics/mechanics/energy/heatAndTemperature/heatAndTemperature.html|url-status=dead}}</ref>{{better source|date=December 2024}}

==State changes==
A gas can go through two different state changes. If the temperature is low enough the gas can [[Condensation|condense]] and turn into a liquid. Sometimes, if the temperature is low enough it can go through [[Deposition (phase transition)|deposition]], where it changes straight to a solid. Normally a gas must first condense to a liquid, and then [[Freezing|freeze]] to become a solid, but if the temperature is very low it can skip the liquid stage and instantly become solid. Frost on the ground in winter is caused by this. [[Water vapour]] – a gas – goes into the air which is very cold, and instantly becomes [[ice]] due to deposition.{{cn|date=December 2024}}

