The original '''World Trade Center''' ('''WTC''') was a complex of seven buildings in [[Lower Manhattan]], [[New York City]], [[New York (state)|New York]], [[United States]]. It was destroyed in the [[September 11 Attacks|September 11 attacks]]; a [[World Trade Center (2001–present)|new complex]] is mostly complete and is built on the [[World Trade Center site|site]] of the old one.

The biggest buildings in the original World Trade Center were the '''Twin Towers'''. The North Tower was the tallest building in the world when it was built. Taller ones were built later. The Twin Towers had 110 stories, and, at the time of their destruction, were the two tallest in New York. The other buildings in the complex were the [[Marriott World Trade Center]] (3 WTC), 4 WTC, 5 WTC, 6 WTC and and 7 WTC. 

The original World Trade Center was designed by Minoru Yamasaki in the early 1960s using a tube-frame structural design for the twin 110-story towers. In gaining approval for the project, the Port Authority of [[New York (state)|New York]] and [[New Jersey]] agreed to take over the Hudson & Manhattan Railroad which became the Port Authority Trans-Hudson (PATH).

Groundbreaking for the World Trade Center took place on August 5, 1966. The North Tower, officially World Trade Center 1, was completed in December 1970 and the South Tower, officially World Trade Center 2, was finished in July 1971. Construction of the World Trade Center involved excavating a large amount of material that was then used in making [[Battery Park City]] on the west side of [[Lower Manhattan]].

